{
  "about": [
    "The objects the Rust and the C++ reference hosts agree on, byte for byte (docs/SCE_HOST_FEATURES.md,",
    "section 10): each host signs every `sign1` case and seals every `encrypt0` case with the keys below and",
    "the case's nonce, and must write `cose` exactly; each host verifies every `sign1` object under the trust",
    "set of both signing keys and opens every `encrypt0` object, and must answer the case's payload or",
    "plaintext. An object either host writes is then one the other verifies or opens, in either direction.",
    "",
    "Every hexadecimal value is the bytes it spells. `seed` is the 32-byte seed of an Ed25519 key and",
    "`public_key` the key RFC 8032 section 5.1.5 derives from it, which a Rust trust set holds and a C++ trust",
    "set names by the signing key's id. `key` is a 32-byte ChaCha20/Poly1305 key. `nonce` is the 96 bits a",
    "seal draws from its host's random source, which each test fixes for the case. Each object's key id is",
    "the bytes of the case's `key_id`.",
    "",
    "The first payload is the deterministic CBOR of the record {id: 7, label: \"thermostat\"}, and the first",
    "seal's plaintext the first signed object, sealed after it is signed as docs/adr/0107 D9 orders. The",
    "second case of each holds bytes past U+007F, which cross a request and a result as their Latin-1 text;",
    "the third holds 300 bytes, which a byte string's two-byte length head carries.",
    "",
    "The objects were computed by an implementation that shares nothing with either host (the Python",
    "`cryptography` package over OpenSSL, with a CBOR writer of its own), which first reproduced the objects",
    "the Rust host's own tests hold for the COSE working group's key `11` and key `sec-256`."
  ],
